{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_wbsj3a2m6mma5fup35n7rwx2gq3toez377wrpxfajepounu3ovlq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. Summary of the work:\n\n## Result\n\n**One real defect found and fixed**, with all four completion gates now passing.\n\n### The library\nA single C translation unit (`c_src/src/lib.c`, 181 lines) exporting 9 functions: `shift_array`, `process_string`, `apply_bitmask`, `init_matrix`, `compare_allocations`, `arity4`, `arity3`, `arity2`, `arity`.\n\n### Phase A artifacts (created)\n- **`SYMBOLS.md`** — 9 C symbols / 9 Rust symbols, diff empty, no untranslated modules, no stubs.\n- **`ERRORS.md`** — 13 rejection rows derived by grepping every `return`/guard/`switch default`/`NULL` in the C.\n- **`CONFIGS.md`** — 32 configuration rows from the axes the C actually branches on.\n\n### The defect (Phase C, ERRORS.md Note C)\n`process_string(NULL)`: **C died with SIGSEGV (11), Rust died with SIGABRT (6)**. The dev profile's debug assertions inject a UB check that panics with `null pointer dereference occurred`; since the exports are `extern \"C\"`, that panic is non-unwinding and aborts. An external C consumer can observe this, so it was a genuine divergence. Fixed in `Cargo.toml` (`[profile.dev] debug-assertions = false, overflow-checks = false`) so the cdylib adds no Rust-only traps in any profile. **`src/lib.rs` needed no changes** — verified byte-identical to the original translation via `diff`.\n\n### Two things worth flagging\n1. **`arity` has an ABI trap.** The header declares `int len` but the definition takes `unsigned char`. I confirmed by disassembling the shipped C `.so` (`mov %al,-0x4(%rbp)`; `cmpb`/`ja`) that the callee truncates to 8 bits and compares **unsigned** — so `arity(256, …)` returns `-1` while `arity(-1, …)` dispatches to `arity4`. The Rust reproduces this; tested across all 256 low bytes plus 2 000 random `i32`s.\n2. **`compare_allocations` is not a pure function.** It compares two `malloc` addresses, and glibc's LIFO tcache makes consecutive calls alternate (`11, 12, 11, 12…`) *identically in both libraries*. A naive interleaved `assert_eq!(c(x), rust(x))` therefore reports a **false** failure. Per-call rows use parity-neutral 2-call batches; the 2 000-call sequence row runs each library in its **own fresh process** — counting calls and in-process probing were both tried and are unsound (ERRORS.md Note D).\n\n### Verification status\n| Gate | Status |\n|---|---|\n| `nm -D` symbol parity | 9/9, 0 missing, 0 undefined application symbols |\n| Phase B — 32 `CONFIGS.md` rows | all pass, 0 unchecked |\n| Phase C — 13 `ERRORS.md` rows | all pass, 0 unchecked |\n| Every feature combination | `--no-default-features`, default, `--all-features` — 47 tests each, all pass (also verified under `--release`) |\n\nFeature enumeration was automated (`check_all_features.sh`, `run_tests.sh`); the C source has no `#ifdef`/CMake `option()`, so there is exactly one build configuration.\n\nI also **mutation-tested the harness** to prove it isn't passing vacuously: 12 of 13 injected bugs were caught (including the classic `wrapping_rem` → `rem_euclid` negative-modulo bug and removing the `len` truncation). The one survivor is a provably equivalent mutant (`positions > 0` → `>= 0`, where the memmove copies onto itself and the zero-fill loop runs zero times)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1113,"cache_read_input_tokens":154235,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1113,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b6e5c8d3-08a9-40d1-ae2c-c44ea27f6f01","uuid":"c99c53df-3411-4dac-bca0-f25cfaf13b44","timestamp":"2026-08-19T17:53:49.957Z"}
